 ("p", "Note that the opposite of 'must' in deduction is **can't / cannot**, not 'mustn't'. *The lights are on, so he must be in. He can't be asleep.*"),
 ("h3", "Need"),
 ("ul", ["**Need as a modal** is used in negatives and questions: *You needn't go. Need I go?* (no -s, no to)", "**Need as an ordinary verb** takes to and -s: *He needs to rest. Do you need to go? I don't need to know.*", "**Needn't have + past participle** = did something that was not necessary: *You needn't have bought it. I already have one.* **Didn't need to** = it wasn't necessary and we don't say if done: *I didn't need to hurry.*", "**Need** is also followed by a gerund / passive infinitive for things: *The car needs repairing / to be repaired.*"]),
 ("h2", "Used to; Would; Be used to; Get used to"),
 ("table", ["Form", "Meaning", "Example"], [
  ["used to + base verb", "past habit or past state that no longer exists", "She used to live in Jaipur. We used to play cricket daily."],
  ["didn't use to / never used to", "negative (questions: Did you use to ... ?)", "I didn't use to like tea."],
  ["be used to + noun / -ing", "be accustomed to", "I am used to getting up early."],
  ["get used to + noun / -ing", "become accustomed to", "He is getting used to the cold."]]),
 ("p", "In a question or negative with 'did', the spelling is **use to** (without d): *Did you use to smoke?* A frequent editing error."),
 ("h2", "Dare and Had Better"),
 ("ul", ["**Dare** as a modal in negatives/questions: *He dare not speak. How dare you!* As an ordinary verb: *He did not dare to speak. I dare you to jump.*", "**Had better + base verb**: strong advice with a warning: *You had better hurry or you will miss the bus.* Negative: *had better not go.* It refers to the present or future, though it has 'had'.", "**Be able to**: replaces can in other tenses and when no modal is possible: *She will be able to join us. He has been able to swim for years.*", "**Be going to**: plan or prediction: *I am going to buy a bicycle. It is going to rain.*", "**Be to**: arrangement or order: *The Prime Minister is to visit Japan next week. You are to stay here.*"]),
 ("h2", "Modals in the Past (Perfect Modals)"),
 ("table", ["Form", "Meaning", "Example"], [
  ["could have + V3", "was able to but did not", "I could have won, but I was careless."],
  ["couldn't have + V3", "it was impossible that", "She couldn't have seen him; she was abroad."],
  ["may / might have + V3", "perhaps, uncertain past", "He may have lost his way."],
  ["might have + V3", "unreal past (did not happen) / criticism", "You might have told me!"],
  ["must have + V3", "sure that something happened", "It must have rained last night; the road is wet."],
  ["can't have + V3", "sure it did not happen", "He can't have stolen it."],
  ["should have + V3", "right thing not done (regret / criticism)", "You should have started earlier."],
  ["shouldn't have + V3", "wrong thing done", "You shouldn't have shouted at her."],
  ["needn't have + V3", "did something unnecessary", "You needn't have waited."],
  ["would have + V3", "result in the third conditional", "If I had known, I would have helped."]]),
 ("h2", "Modals in the Passive Voice"),
 ("p", "Pattern: **modal + be + V3**. *The work must be finished today. The gate can be opened from inside. Rules should be obeyed. The letter ought to have been posted yesterday.* (More in Chapter 12.)"),
